1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,180
August 1951. A nameless ridge north of Yanggu,

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,763
in the mountains of eastern Korea.

3
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:08,900
After days and nights of shelling,

4
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:12,251
not a single tree was left standing on its crest.

5
00:00:12,641 --> 00:00:14,727
Yet the moment the guns fell silent,

6
00:00:14,747 --> 00:00:16,400
enemy machine guns opened

7
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:18,232
up again from the bare earth.

8
00:00:18,355 --> 00:00:20,248
Bunkers buried deep in the ridge had

9
00:00:20,268 --> 00:00:22,776
ridden out the entire bombardment.

10
00:00:23,162 --> 00:00:24,976
In the end, men had to crawl up the

11
00:00:24,996 --> 00:00:27,138
slopes with rifles and grenades.

12
00:00:27,278 --> 00:00:28,964
For every bunker they destroyed,

13
00:00:28,984 --> 00:00:30,852
someone fell on that slope.

14
00:00:31,401 --> 00:00:33,722
For this one ridge, South Korean and

15
00:00:33,742 --> 00:00:38,218
UN casualties would climb past 2,800.

16
00:00:38,294 --> 00:00:39,959
An American military newspaper

17
00:00:39,979 --> 00:00:41,917
gave it a chilling name.

18
00:00:42,307 --> 00:00:43,618
Bloody Ridge.

19
00:00:43,734 --> 00:00:46,002
This is the story of a three-week battle

20
00:00:46,022 --> 00:00:48,548
fought in the shadow of the truce talks.

21
00:00:48,941 --> 00:00:51,313
By the summer of 1951, the war had

22
00:00:51,333 --> 00:00:54,659
ground to a halt near the 38th Parallel.

23
00:00:54,759 --> 00:00:56,731
On July 10th, truce talks

24
00:00:56,751 --> 00:00:58,777
finally opened at Kaesong.

25
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:01,022
But while the negotiators haggled,

26
00:01:01,042 --> 00:01:03,379
the guns at the front never stopped.

27
00:01:03,535 --> 00:01:05,098
Every yard of ground held

28
00:01:05,118 --> 00:01:06,817
was leverage at the table.

29
00:01:07,214 --> 00:01:09,202
The UN's main line of resistance

30
00:01:09,222 --> 00:01:11,080
was called the Kansas Line.

31
00:01:11,139 --> 00:01:13,577
In the rugged mountains north of Yanggu,

32
00:01:13,597 --> 00:01:16,851
the US 2nd Infantry Division held the line.

33
00:01:17,241 --> 00:01:19,892
The problem was a ridge just to the north.

34
00:01:20,031 --> 00:01:21,969
North Korean observers hidden on

35
00:01:21,989 --> 00:01:23,414
it were calling down accurate

36
00:01:23,434 --> 00:01:26,080
artillery fire on UN positions.

37
00:01:26,476 --> 00:01:28,311
Eighth Army commander General James

38
00:01:28,331 --> 00:01:30,678
Van Fleet ordered the ridge taken.

39
00:01:30,847 --> 00:01:32,784
He wanted the enemy's eyes put out,

40
00:01:32,804 --> 00:01:35,629
and South Korean units seasoned in battle.

41
00:01:36,039 --> 00:01:38,788
In late July, the US 38th Infantry

42
00:01:38,808 --> 00:01:40,518
had set up a patrol base on

43
00:01:40,538 --> 00:01:44,740
Hill 1179, Taeu-san, to the east.

44
00:01:44,807 --> 00:01:46,711
But heavy monsoon rains washed

45
00:01:46,731 --> 00:01:48,726
out the roads, and the main attack

46
00:01:48,746 --> 00:01:50,960
was put off until mid-August.

47
00:01:51,352 --> 00:01:53,369
The objective: three peaks on

48
00:01:53,389 --> 00:01:55,452
a ridge running east to west.

49
00:01:55,507 --> 00:02:01,018
From west to east, Hills 983, 940, and 773.

50
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:04,489
Hill 983, the highest, looked straight

51
00:02:04,509 --> 00:02:07,129
down on the UN lines to the south.

52
00:02:07,215 --> 00:02:09,210
The ridge was knife-edge narrow,

53
00:02:09,230 --> 00:02:12,526
with sheer slopes falling away on both sides.

54
00:02:12,917 --> 00:02:14,951
The North Koreans had studded the ridge with

55
00:02:14,971 --> 00:02:17,909
log bunkers buried under earth and rock.

56
00:02:18,064 --> 00:02:20,070
US records say the largest could

57
00:02:20,090 --> 00:02:22,091
hold as many as sixty men.

58
00:02:22,496 --> 00:02:24,908
Trenches linked bunker to bunker,

59
00:02:24,928 --> 00:02:27,787
and minefields covered the approaches.

60
00:02:27,846 --> 00:02:30,671
When the shells came, they took cover; when

61
00:02:30,691 --> 00:02:33,986
the shelling lifted, the guns came back out.

62
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:39,004
On August 18th, 1951, the ROK 36th Regiment

63
00:02:39,024 --> 00:02:41,848
of the 5th Division led the first assault.

64
00:02:41,958 --> 00:02:43,520
It was a South Korean regiment

65
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:45,867
attached to the US 2nd Division.

66
00:02:46,265 --> 00:02:47,455
Before the assault,

67
00:02:47,475 --> 00:02:49,912
UN artillery pounded the ridge.

68
00:02:50,036 --> 00:02:51,734
Several artillery battalions,

69
00:02:51,754 --> 00:02:54,398
heavy mortars, and tanks: every gun

70
00:02:54,418 --> 00:02:56,731
in range was brought to bear.

71
00:02:57,117 --> 00:02:59,032
Days of shelling turned the green

72
00:02:59,052 --> 00:03:01,513
ridge into bare, pale earth.

73
00:03:01,609 --> 00:03:03,901
But when the barrage lifted, the enemy

74
00:03:03,921 --> 00:03:06,438
guns came back to life underground.

75
00:03:06,824 --> 00:03:09,695
As the attackers climbed, the defenders threw

76
00:03:09,715 --> 00:03:12,517
and rolled grenades down on their heads.

77
00:03:12,641 --> 00:03:13,838
With every step upward,

78
00:03:13,858 --> 00:03:16,616
more grenades came tumbling down.

79
00:03:17,014 --> 00:03:18,724
After five days of repeated

80
00:03:18,744 --> 00:03:21,217
frontal assaults, the 36th Regiment

81
00:03:21,237 --> 00:03:23,424
finally stood on the ridge.

82
00:03:23,542 --> 00:03:24,842
But a fierce counterattack

83
00:03:24,862 --> 00:03:26,306
threw them back off it.

84
00:03:26,695 --> 00:03:29,107
As casualties mounted, the exhausted

85
00:03:29,127 --> 00:03:31,355
men's morale began to crack.

86
00:03:31,382 --> 00:03:33,752
On August 27th, some units of the

87
00:03:33,772 --> 00:03:36,497
36th Regiment broke and fell back.

88
00:03:36,889 --> 00:03:38,413
The panic spread to elements of the

89
00:03:38,433 --> 00:03:41,407
US 9th Infantry, sent up in support.

90
00:03:41,512 --> 00:03:44,725
That same day, the 9th's 2nd Battalion struck

91
00:03:44,745 --> 00:03:48,406
at Hill 983, and it too was forced back.

92
00:03:48,795 --> 00:03:52,558
The next day, the 3rd Battalion went for Hill 773,

93
00:03:52,578 --> 00:03:55,142
and was driven off by a night attack.

94
00:03:55,240 --> 00:03:56,665
It was the attackers who were being

95
00:03:56,685 --> 00:03:59,694
worn down, one battalion at a time.

96
00:04:00,093 --> 00:04:02,647
On August 30th, the 9th Infantry hit

97
00:04:02,667 --> 00:04:06,231
Hill 940, in the center, head-on.

98
00:04:06,291 --> 00:04:07,489
They got within a few hundred

99
00:04:07,509 --> 00:04:09,526
yards of the crest before bunker

100
00:04:09,546 --> 00:04:11,605
machine guns stopped them again.

101
00:04:11,986 --> 00:04:14,472
Company A was cut to half strength,

102
00:04:14,492 --> 00:04:17,919
and its commander, Lieutenant Dunn, was killed.

103
00:04:18,046 --> 00:04:20,473
Lieutenant Morrow, the artillery observer

104
00:04:20,493 --> 00:04:23,326
beside him, took command on the spot.

105
00:04:23,706 --> 00:04:25,222
And so the ridge was soaked

106
00:04:25,242 --> 00:04:27,316
in the blood of both sides.

107
00:04:27,377 --> 00:04:29,497
The military newspaper Stars and

108
00:04:29,517 --> 00:04:32,079
Stripes called it Bloody Ridge.

109
00:04:32,127 --> 00:04:33,666
For security it never named the

110
00:04:33,686 --> 00:04:35,225
place, so the men fighting

111
00:04:35,245 --> 00:04:36,960
there didn't know it was them.

112
00:04:37,347 --> 00:04:40,688
On August 28th, the new X Corps commander,

113
00:04:40,708 --> 00:04:44,216
General Clovis Byers, changed the plan.

114
00:04:44,343 --> 00:04:46,816
Instead of hammering one ridge, the whole

115
00:04:46,836 --> 00:04:49,377
corps front would push forward at once.

116
00:04:49,762 --> 00:04:51,945
The ROK 5th and 1st Marine

117
00:04:51,965 --> 00:04:54,256
Divisions drove on the Punchbowl;

118
00:04:54,276 --> 00:04:57,203
the ROK 7th, west of the ridge.

119
00:04:57,323 --> 00:04:59,021
The enemy could no longer mass his

120
00:04:59,041 --> 00:05:01,272
guns and reserves in one place.

121
00:05:01,667 --> 00:05:04,052
On August 31st, the Marines met

122
00:05:04,072 --> 00:05:05,509
little resistance and reached

123
00:05:05,529 --> 00:05:07,648
the Punchbowl's northern rim.

124
00:05:07,744 --> 00:05:09,522
The flank of the North Koreans on the

125
00:05:09,542 --> 00:05:12,057
ridge was slowly being laid bare.

126
00:05:12,453 --> 00:05:15,541
Meanwhile, the 1st Battalion, 9th Infantry,

127
00:05:15,561 --> 00:05:19,296
broke the bunkers on Hill 773 one by one.

128
00:05:19,422 --> 00:05:21,689
One platoon leader, his radio dead,

129
00:05:21,709 --> 00:05:24,141
took a bunker with nothing but grenades.

130
00:05:24,539 --> 00:05:27,549
On September 3rd, after napalm and bombs

131
00:05:27,569 --> 00:05:30,862
swept the ridge, the attack went in again.

132
00:05:30,979 --> 00:05:33,828
Company C, about eighty-five men strong,

133
00:05:33,848 --> 00:05:35,984
came out of it with about thirty.

134
00:05:36,394 --> 00:05:39,681
Then the US 23rd and 38th Infantry swung

135
00:05:39,701 --> 00:05:42,036
wide around both ends of the ridge.

136
00:05:42,135 --> 00:05:43,800
With the 9th still pressing the

137
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:46,048
front, it was a double envelopment

138
00:05:46,068 --> 00:05:48,030
closing from three sides.

139
00:05:48,420 --> 00:05:51,060
On September 4th and 5th, the 2nd Division

140
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:53,496
finally reached the top of the ridge.

141
00:05:53,564 --> 00:05:55,839
But the crest was strangely quiet.

142
00:05:56,223 --> 00:05:57,637
The North Koreans had already

143
00:05:57,657 --> 00:05:59,195
slipped away to the north.

144
00:05:59,335 --> 00:06:01,727
Left behind on the ridge were abandoned

145
00:06:01,747 --> 00:06:05,099
supplies and more than five hundred enemy dead.

146
00:06:05,501 --> 00:06:07,250
Per the US Army official history,

147
00:06:07,270 --> 00:06:11,358
South Korean and UN casualties topped 2,800.

148
00:06:11,449 --> 00:06:14,513
By Korean records, the ROK 36th Regiment

149
00:06:14,533 --> 00:06:17,108
alone lost more than a thousand men.

150
00:06:17,206 --> 00:06:19,200
The 2nd Division estimated enemy

151
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:21,570
losses at over fifteen thousand.

152
00:06:21,935 --> 00:06:24,572
Why did one ridge cost so much blood?

153
00:06:24,664 --> 00:06:27,120
Let's break it down into four factors.

154
00:06:27,517 --> 00:06:30,050
First, the underground fortress.

155
00:06:30,451 --> 00:06:32,637
The bunkers were heavy timbers piled with

156
00:06:32,657 --> 00:06:35,801
layer upon layer of earth and stone.

157
00:06:35,929 --> 00:06:37,968
Unless a heavy shell landed squarely

158
00:06:37,988 --> 00:06:40,509
on top, they would not collapse.

159
00:06:40,892 --> 00:06:43,196
Second, the limits of firepower.

160
00:06:43,587 --> 00:06:46,249
The UN owned the sky and the artillery.

161
00:06:46,304 --> 00:06:49,641
The 155-millimeter Long Tom was the best

162
00:06:49,661 --> 00:06:52,305
bunker-buster, but there were few of them.

163
00:06:52,420 --> 00:06:54,484
Digging the enemy out of his bunkers was,

164
00:06:54,504 --> 00:06:56,931
in the end, a job for the infantry.

165
00:06:57,328 --> 00:07:00,198
Third, the price of the frontal assault.

166
00:07:00,588 --> 00:07:02,062
On a ridge that narrow,

167
00:07:02,082 --> 00:07:04,145
there was no room to spread out.

168
00:07:04,255 --> 00:07:06,397
Men climbing in single file walked

169
00:07:06,417 --> 00:07:08,536
straight into the bunkers' fire.

170
00:07:08,625 --> 00:07:10,563
Some platoons went into the attack

171
00:07:10,583 --> 00:07:12,339
made up mostly of replacements

172
00:07:12,359 --> 00:07:14,724
who had only just arrived.

173
00:07:15,122 --> 00:07:17,467
Fourth, the double envelopment.

174
00:07:17,853 --> 00:07:19,572
In the end, the ridge was opened not

175
00:07:19,592 --> 00:07:22,099
from the front, but from the flanks.

176
00:07:22,222 --> 00:07:24,660
Squeezed along the whole front and from

177
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:27,733
both flanks, the enemy gave it up himself.

178
00:07:28,143 --> 00:07:30,382
But the North Koreans had fallen back only

179
00:07:30,402 --> 00:07:33,977
a few miles, to another ridge to the north.

180
00:07:34,024 --> 00:07:37,134
And on September 13th, the 2nd Division went

181
00:07:37,154 --> 00:07:40,114
on the attack again, toward that ridge.

182
00:07:40,504 --> 00:07:41,721
That place would come to be

183
00:07:41,741 --> 00:07:44,112
called Heartbreak Ridge.

184
00:07:44,161 --> 00:07:45,735
Bloody Ridge was only the beginning of

185
00:07:45,755 --> 00:07:48,253
a longer, harsher war for the hills.

186
00:07:48,647 --> 00:07:51,113
Today, a memorial to the Battle of Bloody

187
00:07:51,133 --> 00:07:54,982
Ridge stands in Worun-ri, Yanggu County.

188
00:07:55,078 --> 00:07:57,130
On those slopes, the search for those who

189
00:07:57,150 --> 00:08:00,127
never came home has gone on for years.

190
00:08:00,673 --> 00:08:02,808
A single line on the negotiating table

191
00:08:02,828 --> 00:08:05,902
cost thousands of lives on this ridge.

192
00:08:05,953 --> 00:08:08,552
The ground they held is today's Yanggu,

193
00:08:08,572 --> 00:08:10,670
south of the armistice line.

194
00:08:11,228 --> 00:08:14,271
A nameless ridge north of Yanggu.

195
00:08:14,359 --> 00:08:17,427
To this day, its name remains Bloody Ridge.

196
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:19,494
If this story meant something to you,

197
00:08:19,514 --> 00:08:21,872
please subscribe and like the video.

198
00:08:21,892 --> 00:08:23,000
We'll continue to bring the

199
00:08:23,020 --> 00:08:24,948
forgotten records of war to light.
